undation, London, 1971, 60 pp, studies directed by Felix Walter. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 UPDATE: THE NAMED NEXT ROUTE IS NOW CLOSED, AND THE ROW GAINS THE HOLDINGS LAYER THAT MAKES DOCUMENT DELIVERY POSSIBLE. This row''s previous re-description ended ''NEXT ROUTE, UNTRIED: Jisc Library Hub Discover, and the Disabled Living Foundation successor organisation.'' JISC WAS TRIED THIS BATCH AND IS UNREACHABLE: two queries, both HTTP 403 carrying a Cloudflare ''Just a moment...'' interstitial, both persisted. NOTHING IS ESTABLISHED ABOUT WHAT JISC HOLDS -- blocked route, not a null -- and it is the second discovery catalogue in two batches to fail this way after HathiTrust. The row should stop naming Jisc as its next step. NOTHING IS RETRACTED FROM THE EARLIER DESCRIPTION: I checked whether batch 18 had missed the Open Library record before writing anything, and it had not -- Felix Walter, DLF, London, 1971, 60 pp is already correctly recorded above, and the corporate-versus-directing-author distinction it draws stands. WHAT IS NEW IS THE IDENTIFIER SET, from the same Open Library edition record OL4653562M re-read this batch: ISBN-10 0901908053, LCCN 77502052, OCLC 222208, LC classification NA2545.P5 D5, Dewey 729/.2, one edition only, publisher address 346 Kensington High St, London W.14, source record marc:marc_loc_2016 -- a LIBRARY OF CONGRESS MARC record, which is why the 1971 date is authoritative rather than inferred, and which independently corroborates the year REF-01005''s Table 13 footnote c gives while that bibliography entry''s own year digits are OCR-damaged. WHY THE IDENTIFIERS ARE THE POINT: GAP-026 says what this needs is a library or document-delivery route and that this is ''a different kind of work from a retrieval ladder''. An OCLC number and an LCCN are exactly what converts that from a search into a request -- interlibrary loan on OCLC 222208, or a direct approach to a holding library. NO SEARCH THIS ENVIRONMENT